id: E543-draft-2301_80739235
trigger_tool: binary-sign-tool|OHOS ELF|consumer execution|Error 126|codesign
phase: target_prepare|target_launch|consumer_validation
error_signature: 'Permission denied|Error 126|\.codesign section missing|signing tool unavailable'
severity: 2
category: ohos|signing|permissions|target-validation|consumer
match_keywords: OHOS ELF, binary-sign-tool, application release alias, owner execute, task-private copy, Error 126
root_cause: A target runner can receive a valid AArch64 ELF and still fail with RC126 or EPERM when the task-private executable was signed with a host-only certificate path or when the target signer is absent; a signature section check alone also does not establish target trust or executable permission.
affected_scope: OpenHarmony PC tasks that transfer ELF executables into a task-owned runtime directory and execute them on the target device.
verification_cmd: Freeze the official host inspection and device signer closure by bytes/SHA-256; transfer the native OpenHarmony signer into the task root; sign task-private copies on-device with selfSign; verify AArch64 ELF identity, one valid .codesign block, changed output bytes, owner execute mode after transfer, and an exact target consumer run.
rec_priority: 1
rec_approach: task-private-device-signer-closure-and-consumer-proof
rec_description: Treat signing as an immutable input closure. Use the official native OpenHarmony binary-sign-tool on the target with selfSign=1, sign only task-private copies, and verify the signed output before execution. A host JAR/certificate result can be useful structural evidence but is not a substitute for the target signer. On the target, set owner/group/other execute bits and prove the installed consumer executes as the same owner. Do not replay a consumed launch after a signer or payload change; create a successor run.
rec_command: 'binary-sign-tool sign -selfSign 1 -inFile pending.elf -outFile signed.elf; chmod 755 signed.elf; binary-sign-tool display-sign -inFile signed.elf'
rec_auto_fix: false
rec_impl: target-preparation
fallback_priority: 2
fallback_approach: fail-closed
fallback_description: If the signer closure cannot be verified or the target consumer still returns RC126, record a terminal failure and create a new immutable successor after the precise cause is repaired; never turn an unsigned or mode-only local result into target PASS.
fallback_command: 'preserve failed run evidence, release only its task-owned remote root, regenerate the signed payload and runner, and perform one fresh transfer/prepare/launch'
fallback_auto_fix: false
fallback_impl: transaction
forbid_1_pattern: 'reuse.*unsigned|signer.*missing.*PASS'
forbid_1_reason: A successful local build does not prove target trust or executable permission.
forbid_1_consequence: The first real consumer run can fail with RC126 after all upstream tests pass.
forbid_2_pattern: 'replay.*launch|second.*launch'
forbid_2_reason: A consumed launch is immutable even when the failure is caused by a replaceable input.
forbid_2_consequence: Evidence from different payloads or launch identities becomes unverifiable.
principles: P007, P018, P020
causes:
caused_by:
similar_to: E077
